@ Index:   Load line zones (IMO Load Lines 1966, Annex II) on a world map for a date
@ Author:  Victor Valdes
@ Version: 0.1.0
@ Source:  https://github.com/valdesvj/zones47
@ Tested:  C47 simulator (python/c47sim.py): tests/test_load47.py, every area and season against the zones of
@ Tested:  tools/make_loadlines.py (46 CFR 42.30, MSC.329(90)) at many dates
@ Input:   none: the date from the clock (7 and 9 move it 15 days)
@ Output:  the map with the summer, tropical and winter zones of the date; under it the cell of the cursor:
@ Output:  latitude and longitude, its zone on the date, the area and its seasons
@
@ ---- What it is ----
@ Load 47 shows the load line zones and seasonal areas of the world (IMO International Convention on Load Lines
@ 1966, Annex II, as amended) on a dot map, for a date: which waters are in the Summer, Tropical or Winter zone that
@ day, for ships over 100 m. A cursor gives a cell's latitude and longitude, its zone on the date, the area and its
@ seasons. INDICATIVE ONLY: 3 degrees a cell; the official boundaries are those of Annex II and its chart.
@
@ ---- How to use it ----
@ Load LOAD47 and press XEQ LOAD47. It starts on the calculator's date.
@   8 4 6 2   move the cursor (around at the edges)
@   7 9       15 days back / on (the zones are drawn again where they change)
@   1         back to the clock's date
@   0         the end
@ On the map: a tropical sea cell has one dot, a winter one two dots on a diagonal, summer nothing; land is solid.
@
@ ---- What it changes on your calculator ----
@ Your stack and LASTx are given back at the end. It uses the registers R20-R92 and clears them at the end; R00-R19
@ and R93-R99 are yours. No variable or matrix is left behind. GRMOD 0 and GRFNT 20 are set at the end.
@
@ ---- Programs and labels ----
@ One program, LOAD47, with one global label (LOAD47). The areas (LBL 01-nn), the cursor's rows (LBL 50-94, called
@ with XEQ IND) and every routine are local labels. Written with the STRUCT commands, ATEXT and GRFNT of the C47 / R47
@ firmware (00.109.05.00a0.ALPHA of 5 Oct 2026 or later).
@
@ ---- Technical notes (from the commented source) ----
@ LOAD47 v0.1.0 - Load 47: the load line zones of the world on the C47 / R47
@ The International Convention on Load Lines 1966 (IMO), Annex II: zones, areas and seasonal periods
@ (regulation 47 as amended by MSC.329(90)); ships over 100 m. The dot map of ZONE47 (3 degrees a dot,
@ 81N to 54S); each 3-degree cell takes the area of its centre (tools/make_loadlines.py -> python/loadzones.py).
@ On the date (the clock's, or moved with 7 and 9) every area is drawn in its zone: tropical a dot in each
@ sea cell, winter two dots on a diagonal, summer nothing (land: the full 2 x 2 dot). A cursor (8 4 6 2)
@ shows its cell: latitude and longitude of the centre, the zone on the date, the area and its seasons.
@ INDICATIVE ONLY: 3 degrees a cell; the official boundaries are those of Annex II and its chart.
@ Keys: 8 4 6 2 the cursor (around at the edges), 7 15 days back, 9 15 days on, 1 the clock's date again,
@ 0 the end. The user's stack and LASTx are kept; at the end R20-R92 are cleared.
@ Screen 400 x 240, row 0 = bottom. Dot row i (0 the top) at rows 194 - 3i and 193 - 3i, dot column j at
@ x 20 + 3j and 21 + 3j (as ZONE47). The areas: LBL 01-nn (written by the build), each: its name STO 72,
@ its season text STO 73, its zone outside the seasons (0 summer, 1 tropical, 2 winter) STO 74, the seasons
@ (start MMDD, end MMDD, zone) STO 75-80 (none: 0), then XEQ :ZNOW:, RCL 90, X=0?, RTN (no change: no
@ drawing) and its pieces: L, n, row, x and XEQ :PC: each (n dot columns from x, the band from that row, L the
@ lower rows of its sea cells, worked out by the build). The cursor's area: the row labels LBL 50-94 (runs of areas).
@ The zones drawn are kept in R89 (base 3, a digit an area): a new date draws again, in XOR, only the
@ areas whose zone changed.
@ Registers: R20-R22 the map bands, R25 x, R30 the date's JD (noon), R31 the days moved, R32 the cursor row
@ i, R33 the key, R34 the text, R35 the cursor column j, R36 the mode (0 draw, 2 the texts of an area),
@ R37 the cursor's area (0: summer zone), R38 the area being run, R39 MMDD of the date, R40 the zone of the
@ area on the date, R41-R53 work, R60-R62 day month year (LBL :CAL:), R72-R80 the area, R88 the day and
@ month names, R89 the zones drawn, R90-R92 the change.
@

LBL "LOAD47"
@ the user's stack in the local registers: STOS R.00 (R.00-R.07, the 8 levels), then LASTx in R.08
LocR 9
STOS R.00
LASTx
STO R.08
XEQ 47                          ; -> :INIT:
@
@ ---- keys: 8 4 6 2 the cursor, 7 9 the date, 1 today, 0 the end ----
DO 01
  DO 02
    PAUSE 50
    KEY? 33
  WHILE 02
  ENDDO 02
  RCL 33
  82
  X≠Y?
WHILE 01
@   8 4 6 2 (53 62 64 73): the row and column steps from the key code, as ELEM47: k div 10 - 6 rows,
@   k mod 10 - 3 columns; |dr| + |dc| = 1 for these four keys only
  RCL 33
  10
  ÷
  IP
  6
  -
  STO 41
  RCL 33
  10
  MOD
  3
  -
  STO 42
  ABS
  RCL 41
  ABS
  +
  1
  X=Y?
  XEQ 00                        ; -> :MOVE:
@   7 (52) 15 days back, 9 (54) 15 days on, 1 (72) the clock's date
  RCL 33
  52
  X=Y?
  XEQ 17                        ; -> :BACK:
  RCL 33
  54
  X=Y?
  XEQ 18                        ; -> :FWD:
  RCL 33
  72
  X=Y?
  XEQ 19                        ; -> :TODAY:
ENDDO 01
@
@ ---- 0: the end: R20-R92 cleared, GRMOD 0, GRFNT 20, the screen; the user's stack and LASTx back ----
20.73
R-CLR
0
GRMOD
20
GRFNT
CLLCD
RCL R.08
STO L
RCLS R.00
RTN
